NeoWiki exposes a programmable surface: a graph database, parser functions, a Lua library, special pages, and a REST API. This page demonstrates each, showing the wikitext source alongside the rendered result.

== Browse ==

* [[Special:NeoWiki]]: the newest Subjects and links to the other NeoWiki pages
* [[Special:Schemas]]: all schemas
* [[Special:Layouts]]: all layouts
* [[Special:Mappings]]: all ontology mappings
* [[Special:Subject/sEpfwJLnxyQy6vR|Special:Subject/&lt;subject id&gt;]]: one Subject, the Subjects it references, and the ones that reference it; the id is in the ID line of the Subject's Data tab row

== Query the graph ==

The <code><nowiki>{{#sparql_raw}}</nowiki></code> parser function runs a SPARQL query and returns the raw JSON.

Source:

<syntaxhighlight lang="mediawiki">
{{#sparql_raw:SELECT ?museum ?visitors WHERE { ?m <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <{{SERVER}}/schema/Museum> . ?m <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> ?museum . ?m <{{SERVER}}/prop/Annual_visitors> ?visitors } ORDER BY DESC(?visitors) LIMIT 5}}
</syntaxhighlight>

Result:

{{#sparql_raw:SELECT ?museum ?visitors WHERE { ?m <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <{{SERVER}}/schema/Museum> . ?m <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> ?museum . ?m <{{SERVER}}/prop/Annual_visitors> ?visitors } ORDER BY DESC(?visitors) LIMIT 5}}

{{#ifexist:SPARQL queries|[[SPARQL queries]] has more examples, from Lua and over REST too.}}

Cypher queries need a Neo4j backend. On a wiki with one, the <code><nowiki>{{#cypher_raw}}</nowiki></code> parser function runs the same query in Cypher:

<syntaxhighlight lang="mediawiki">
{{#cypher_raw:MATCH (m:Museum) RETURN m.name AS name, m.`Annual visitors` AS visitors ORDER BY visitors DESC LIMIT 5}}
</syntaxhighlight>

== Read values in wikitext ==

The <code><nowiki>{{#neowiki_value}}</nowiki></code> parser function reads a single property from a subject on a page.

Source:

<syntaxhighlight lang="mediawiki">
{{#neowiki_value:Annual visitors|page=Rijksmuseum}}
</syntaxhighlight>

Result: {{#neowiki_value:Annual visitors|page=Rijksmuseum}}

Source:

<syntaxhighlight lang="mediawiki">
{{#neowiki_value:Founded at|page=ACME Inc}}
</syntaxhighlight>

Result: {{#neowiki_value:Founded at|page=ACME Inc}}

== Create subjects from wikitext ==

The <code><nowiki>{{#create_subject}}</nowiki></code> parser function renders a button that opens the Subject creator, optionally fixed to a Schema and a target page.

Source:

<syntaxhighlight lang="mediawiki">
{{#create_subject: schema=City | page=new | text=Add a city}}
</syntaxhighlight>

Result:

{{#create_subject: schema=City | page=new | text=Add a city}}

== Read values in Lua ==

The <code>mw.neowiki</code> Lua library exposes structured data to your Scribunto modules. The example below reads a single property and runs a SPARQL query.

[[Module:LuaExample]]:

<syntaxhighlight lang="lua">
local p = {}
local nw = require( 'mw.neowiki' )

function p.foundedYear( frame )
	local year = nw.getValue( 'Founded', { page = frame.args[1] } )
	return tostring( year or '' )
end

function p.largestMuseums( frame )
	if not nw.sparqlQuery then
		return "''This list needs a SPARQL store.''"
	end

	local base = mw.site.server
	local result = nw.sparqlQuery(
		'SELECT ?label ?visitors WHERE { ' ..
		'?m <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <' .. base .. '/schema/Museum> . ' ..
		'?m <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> ?label . ' ..
		'?m <' .. base .. '/prop/Annual_visitors> ?visitors ' ..
		'} ORDER BY DESC(?visitors) LIMIT 3'
	)

	local list = {}
	for _, binding in ipairs( result.results.bindings ) do
		list[#list + 1] = '* ' .. binding.label.value .. ' (' .. binding.visitors.value .. ' visitors)'
	end
	return table.concat( list, '\n' )
end

return p
</syntaxhighlight>

Invoke it from wikitext:

<syntaxhighlight lang="mediawiki">
{{#invoke:LuaExample|foundedYear|Rijksmuseum}}

{{#invoke:LuaExample|largestMuseums}}
</syntaxhighlight>

Result:

Rijksmuseum was founded in {{#invoke:LuaExample|foundedYear|Rijksmuseum}}.

Museums with the most visitors in this demo:

{{#invoke:LuaExample|largestMuseums}}

For the full library reference, see the [https://neowiki.ai/docs/authoring/lua-api Lua API documentation].

== Reactive UI ==

NeoWiki components embed in pages as reactive UIs that update without a page reload. See [[Subject views]] for the same Sandbox subject in three layouts, with edits propagating between them.

== REST API ==

NeoWiki exposes a REST API for reading and writing structured data. The OpenAPI 3.0 spec is auto-generated:

* OpenAPI spec: <code>[{{SERVER}}{{SCRIPTPATH}}/rest.php/specs/v0/module/- /rest.php/specs/v0/module/-]</code>
* Module discovery: <code>[{{SERVER}}{{SCRIPTPATH}}/rest.php/specs/v0/discovery /rest.php/specs/v0/discovery]</code>

Representative read endpoints:

* <code>GET /neowiki/v0/subject/{subjectId}</code>
* <code>GET /neowiki/v0/schema/{schemaName}</code>
* <code>GET /neowiki/v0/schema/{schemaName}/json-schema</code>: a Schema's Subjects as a [https://neowiki.ai/docs/api/json-schema JSON Schema] document, for validating data before upload; try [{{SERVER}}{{SCRIPTPATH}}/rest.php/neowiki/v0/schema/Person/json-schema Person]

For full endpoint documentation, see the OpenAPI spec above.
